Short:        Flite text-to-speech on MNT ZZ9000 ARM Core1
Uploader:     xanxi (xanxi amiga)
Author:       Xanxi
Type:         util/misc
Version:      1.0
Architecture: m68k-amigaos >= 3.0
Distribution: Aminet
Requires:     MNT ZZ9000 with XACP firmware, AHI, 68020+

ZZSpeech turns your Amiga into a talking machine by putting the ARM
Cortex-A9 coprocessor of the MNT ZZ9000 to work.

The 68k sends text, ARM Core1 synthesises the speech with Flite (CMU
Festival Lite), and the audio comes back through AHI. The 68k stays free:
no software synthesis on the m68k side.

Features

  - Resident daemon: the voice is loaded once, then speech is immediate
  - Intuition/GadTools interface with a multi-line text editor
  - Adjustable speaking speed (50% - 200%)
  - Read any text file, or drop its icon on the window
  - Save speech to a standard 16-bit WAV file
  - DOS handler: echo "hello" >ZZSPEECH:  /  copy text.txt TO ZZSPEECH:
  - CLI client (ZZSay) for scripts
  - Interchangeable voices: any CMU Arctic .flitevox file

Requirements

  ZZSpeech needs an MNT ZZ9000 running the XACP firmware, which provides
  the ARM Core1 mailbox protocol. A ZZ9000 with the stock MNT firmware
  will NOT work.
  Also needs AmigaOS 3.x, AHI, and an audio output (Paula or a sound card).

Voices

  slt (2.4 MB) is included as the default voice.
  Other CMU Arctic voices (rms, bdl, clb...) can be downloaded from
  festvox.org/flite and loaded with "Load voice...".

Installation

  See BUILD.txt. In short: put ZZSpeechDaemon, ZZSay, ZZSpeechGUI and
  slt.flitevox in a drawer, ZZSpeech-Handler in L:, the ZZSPEECH mountlist
  in SYS:Storage/DOSDrivers/ and ZZSpeech-Start in S:.

Credits

  Flite (CMU Festival Lite) by Carnegie Mellon University.
  ZZ9000 hardware by MNT Research.
  ZZSpeech, the ARM Core1 port and the XACP firmware by Xanxi, 2026.
